    "note": [
        {
            "structuredValue": [],
            "parallelValue": [],
            "groupedValue": [],
            "value": "In this paper we present our work using terrestrial LiDAR data to provide a tool that constructs well constrained Discrete Frature Neworks (DFNs) in order to investigate the impact of fractures on fluid-flow in porous media. Our goals were to identify and extract exposures of natural fracture planes from data acquired through a terrestrial LiDAR survey, then to construct a DFN of the extracted fractures which honors mechanically-based outcrop observations and is numerically suitable for fluid-flow simulations. We provided two possible methods to extract 3D geometries of natural fractures from an outcrop in LiDAR-based digital format: (1) a manual method and (2) a semi-automated method. Each method captures fractures that have been observed in the field, though their results differ somewhat. Given the 3D geometries of fractures from both extraction methods, we presented a workflow to create a DFN unstructured mesh and performed fluid-flow simulations with the mesh. Our flow results indicate that accounting for multiple sets of fracture orientations and their role in fracture connectivity can breakthrough time.",
            "type": "abstract",
            "identifier": [],
            "note": [],
            "appliesTo": []
        },
